 to you  as a girl dad  wow thank you for that great question um  you know girl dad a dad period i just i  hope that this  inspires people to literally and  figuratively look up i really think  having something to look up to now  especially  as we bring 2020 to a close it's just  something that we all need  and you know you mentioned two people  who mean so much to me and i could name  a ton more but you know coach cal  and dr walsh my advisor in undergrad  were um  huge inspirations to me and so to hear  that they were inspired by this i mean  you're about to make me cry that that  brought tears to my eyes just now  i was fortunate to see a shuttle launch  uh when i was still flying test  uh missions in the navy and and it did  it brought a tear to my eye it was just  such an amazing thing to think  those folks are leaving the planet for  for a while and it's hard again  another thing that's kind of a  life-changing event so to to share that  with them was was truly an amazing  opportunity thanks for the question  wow now we're all crying okay let's go  to tarik malik with space.com  hello and and good morning my question  is for the station veterans and for you  kate  uh i'm curious you know this is uh the  first time we've seen  a seven-member long-duration crew i'm  wondering is it  feel extra crowded uh are there lines at  the galley in the  uh and the the wc i'm just wondering  what you're hoping  with that extra person on board to  accomplish in terms of science and  research for the station  thanks tariq that's a great question and  i would say  it's crowded has got like some negative  connotations  this is busy in a great way there's  energy up here we've got  people zooming by you know hopper's been  working in the glove box  this morning somebody else is in another  module doing science  somebody else is on the exercise machine  so you can tell that there is  just so much going on on the space  station  and by having five of us up here now  we're able to really accomplish a huge  amount  of research and so there's some level of  maintenance that we always have to do on  the space station and so when you're  just  one person like me a lot of time gets  taken up just keeping this place running  now that we've got all of us the amount  of scientific  output that you're going to see from  this mission i think is absolutely  incredible  all right let's go to rachel joy with  florida today  hey everybody it was an amazing launch  thank you so much for  doing this today um we're just dying to  know  how you chose baby yoda as the zero g  indicator  and um how does he compare flying on the  falcon 9 versus the mandalorian  spacecraft thank you  wow yeah so the  you know each crew that's a the the zero  g  indicator is a has a long tradition that  crews have been able to pick those out  and um and so each crew has that  opportunity  and so that was one we we talked about  um we had just  uh started watching the series and um  it's hard not to like baby yoda  and you know it kind of ties in i i  think with um  the name of our spacecraft as well we  talk about resilience and you know it's  been a  it's been a tough year and uh you know  the fact that  they were able to spacex and nasa were  able to get our spacecraft ready to go  the rocket ready to go throughout this  year  throughout the pandemic and all of that  you know we were inspired  by by everybody's effort to do that so  that's why we named resilience  and we hope that it puts a smile on  people's face it  brings hope to them and and so baby yoda  does the same thing  i think everybody when you see him it's  hard not to smile and so it just seemed  appropriate  it seemed appropriate for our crew as  well we like to have a lot of fun  and we have been having a lot of fun um  and so again it was uh it was very  appropriate i think  i think the ride into space was uh was  probably a little rougher than baby yoda  was used to  compared to what he yards normally  okay thank you the next question will be  from nick samuel with vero news  hi everybody this question is for victor  glover  i know that you talked about uh your  first space flight experience  earlier uh so can you talk about how it  finally felt  to make it to the international space  station did you  did you have a sigh of relief or were  you unnerved  can you can you talk about that  wow nick that's a great question and you  know it's  it's so many different feelings it's  hard to explain it it really is hard to  put into words  the the the awe of seeing the space  station even over our video system  from dragon and then feeling the the  soft contact the slow deceleration as we  made contact with the station  and then making full contact and  pressurizing opening the hatch  seeing kate all of these emotions i mean  the happiness the relief but then  knowing that we've got work to do and  and wanting to contribute  and also getting used to space you know  i was i was very slow my brain  is constantly trying to figure out where  up is  and i don't know if it's because i'm a  new guy they made me sleep in the  ceiling so every time i pop my head out  the entire space station is upside down  so i just stay upside down as much as  possible  but that that i think requires extra  processing and so  everything else takes me so much longer  and so i'm trying to push through that  and get work done  but it is an interesting challenge that  i actually find slightly amusing  so it's been a whole bunch of emotions  and honestly i'm still  processing it i've been writing little  notes to myself and things in my journal  and hopefully i'll look back on and be  able to give you a better more concise  answer but  it is so many different feelings um but  but excitement is definitely one of them  that's fantastic okay let's go to mark  caro with  aviation week and space technology  good morning and thank you i want to  follow up on a question to  um to kate earlier and my question  is for uh mike and shannon what what do  you see  uh having five people in the uf segment  now what is your sense of  the environment and and the future of  having that many people  aboard to do all the maintenance and  research  yeah i can uh i'll start off and i'll  just say it's it's go time up here i  mean uh  this you know we came across a hatch  just a couple days ago i guess but it's  it's been pretty busy since we showed up  here so i think  uh the the folks on the ground um all of  the investigators out there  i think they've been waiting a long time  for this moment and  and so they've uh they've got our  calendars full already and i think  it's a good thing we are going to see  just how much science can come out of  this space station and it's a lot  uh but it takes a lot of extra work too  because you know there is limited space  there's limited com capabilities  uh there's limited video capabilities  and so you have to coordinate all of  those kind of things together  and and that's going to be challenging i  think it's going to take us a few  a few weeks to get our feet under us and  figure out how how five people are  actually able to  to work around here without running into  each other the whole time  and all of that but it is pretty  exciting  yeah just to add to that um you know  people have asked about whether or not  we've got  uh uh you know collisions on the  exercise equipment or the whc but really  it's the traffic jams that we have going  through the modules trying to get from  where we need to be from one place to  the other  so it's definitely um you can tell that  it's a lot fuller here  instead of three people on this side  fight people but  everybody's right is we're gonna get a  lot of work done which is really good  for  all the science that goes on in the  world  okay let's take the next question from  robert perlman with collect space  hi um a question for mike hawkins um  you described how dragon performed as a  spacecraft how is it performing as a  bedroom  have you set up camp inside dragon as  you expected and  um i know you had some temperature  issues on the way up getting it warm  enough  have you experienced that again  yeah that's that's a great question and  and to be quite honest um  i'm not quite there yet because the  first thing that we had to do  um after arriving is we had to get all  of the cargo off  and then we had to move a lot of  emergency equipment on board as well and  so  that took us a little bit of time to do  that and i didn't really want to try and  get set up  in dragon until all of that movement was  done and  and i could kind of figure out where the  right place to put the sleeping bag was  and  and a few items i'll take inside there  uh you know as far as the temperature uh  they've  they've been very generous um and kind  of dropped the temperature down a little  bit right where we  are and this happens to be where the  other uh the crew quarters are  and and so that's to make sure that that  our um that resilience stays cool for me  it's probably been a little bit chilly  uh but but i've certainly been  comfortable  we're probably gonna have to talk about  the temperature a little bit more here  um but but overall i think it's gonna  work out just fine um  you know it is a it's a roomy vehicle  and and so um  you know i'm looking forward to to the  next six months  and uh we'll see uh probably it's gonna  take me a few more days to really get  settled in  all right let's go to stephen clark with  space flight now  hi stephen clark for space flight now i  want to follow up on my  an earlier question from chris davenport  for ceviche gucci  um what makes dragon the best and can  you give us a little  window into life aboard the ship for 27  hours on the way to the space station  and how cramped is it compared to uh  your experience with soyuz and space  shuttle  thanks thanks  well uh my other crew member already  described but  the dragon was fun to fly it's uh  it's really kind of uh  they wanted to go dragons really wanted  to go to space  the the engine was really crisp and  clear and the feel the vibration  and definitely defies the gravity and  brings up the space in the matter of  nine minutes or so and uh and the  difference mainly is the  the feel of the all the plumbing all  those the fueling  uh that you actually feel the all the  uh the lucky liquid oxygen going into  the tank  a couple minutes before the flight and  also the  the field the thruster vibration sound  and also the small  uh uh orange orientation control  injectors  since i'm sitting by the window seat i  feel the jet is firing  right next to my head which is actually  a good sign  so uh uh obviously this is uh feels  like you are actually inside the dragon  bringing us up to space so that was  quite a feeling  okay let's take the next question from  jackie goddard with the times of london  hello and congratulations on another  successful crew dragon flight  you'll make space flight look so easy  but we know space is hard  what would be your best tip to future  space travelers  about how to prepare for living and  working in space particularly those  who may not be career astronauts i guess  basically my question  is is there anything important that tom  cruise needs to know  i think if people are going to come to  space and not be career astronauts  i think the biggest tip you could have  is to be  organized because if you're not  organized you're going to lose your  stuff  [Music]  in  okay let's take a question from joey  roulette with reuters  hey uh  i just want to follow up on the bedroom  crew dragon question  um was there any competition for who  gets to sleep in crew dragon and mike  since that'll be you  um what what i guess will your sleeping  arrangement look like and what kind of  tests  and data will you plan to get from that  thanks  yeah uh we were talking about you know  if you know rock paper scissors there's  also another version  called the gunman guerilla man karate  man so  [Laughter]  but uh actually you know there was a  tradition that when the shuttle used to  to come up and bring components and  supplies to the space station i think  the commander always ended up sleeping  on  on the shuttle um and it just it just  felt right that that's uh where i was  supposed to uh to go  um and and so you know we talked about  it as a crew a little bit  um and everybody was uh you know  everybody would be willing to sleep  wherever right you're in space and so  you're not going to complain  you don't want to complain uh about  about where your sleeping quarters are  but uh so  um in terms of you know what we're going  to get out of it  you know most of the time while we're  we're docked dragon is going to be in  what we call a quiescent mode and so  it's completely powered off  um and and so really i think one of the  things that  that i'm becoming more conscious about  as i'm as i'm spending more and more  time in there  is i don't want to i don't do anything  to to damage anything  as i'm going in and out and so um for  160 or  plus days 200 days whatever it is that  we're up here and i'm using that as my  bedroom  it's also what's going to get us home  and so i want to make sure i don't do  anything to compromise that  while i'm sleeping in there so i will  probably spend a lot  more time outside the crew quarters than  if i had or outside dragon  and if i had one of the crew quarters  that are in here so i think that is  that is an important piece of it that i  i don't ever want to forget  okay let's take a question again from  david curley with discovery channel  one more item on that mike uh are you  looking for them to send up some more  quarters i've heard them called if  they're attached to the floor of the  ceiling  coffins as you float over them they kind  of look like coffins phone booths on  them  and suici could you talk about what your  flight and john young mean to you  you've accomplished what your tarot your  mentor  did  okay um i guess i'll start yes there is  there is a plan  uh to have another crew quarters on  board it's called actually casa  um and and so it just it didn't make it  here in time there is a possibility that  that it might show up midway through um  the  the expedition and so if that is the  that is the case then i will uh  be moving out of resilience and and into  that uh crew quarter  and as i mentioned earlier i think  that's a good thing because i think we  want to just make sure we always keep  our  our return vehicles in pristine  condition  and uh and so anyway there is a plan  just not sure when it's going to  actually make it up here for that other  crew quarter  yeah again uh i'm very humbled an honor  to  to fly three vehicles uh uh after mr  john young  and also warri shira but the good thing  is uh many more is  ready to to follow actually in five  months from now the  crew two they have another two more  astronauts who fly the third  uh kind of space vehicle and the race  just begun for the who's gonna be the  first one to fly  four different one so watch out for that  all right let's take another question  from jeff wynn with cbs  los angeles hey victor uh thanks for  doing this one more question for you you  know in recent days you've talked about  social justice  and you've talked about the need for  kids to go into science  as you are circling the earth every 90  minutes what has that done to your  resolve  in terms of social justice and the  debate over extreme climate here  on earth  yeah that's you know jeff that's a great  but very very large question  i'll say this if anything it  strengthened my resolve in all of those  areas because i believe that  you know what what i've said and what  i've tried to work for is just what's  right  to to be honest and upfront with people  and then to work for what's right  and i think that doing the right thing  is always in order so um  you know looking at the planet from this  perspective actually i i went almost a  full day up here before i went and  looked  out the cupola in the daytime to see the  earth and  in these beautiful land masses and  oceans without lines or words drawn on  them  and um and also to look at it at dragon  it's it's beautiful  uh but it just it just heightens an  awareness that you know the planet needs  protection that human life needs  protection  uh and and we are the ones who have to  protect it  all right i think we could talk with you  all day but unfortunately that's all the  time we have for questions thank you so  much for  your time and for your answers today it  was  really great to talk with you we will  continue to keep up with your mission  online at nasa.gov station and by  following the station and nasa  sites on social media thanks for joining  everyone  thank you everyone  station this is houston acr that  concludes our event  thank you to all the participants from  nasa pao station we are now resuming  operational audio communications  [Music]  you
 

